   "source": [
    "## Define & Measure\n",
    "\n",
    "### Q1: Briefly describe the purpose of this analysis\n",
    "\n",The analysis aims to determine if the pour volume at Buster's main tap consistently meets the target of 16.00 oz, considering a lower specification limit (LSL) of 15.80 oz and an upper specification limit (USL) of 16.20 oz. 
    "### Q2: Identify the quality characteristic your team will monitor, and classify its units and measurement scale.\n",
    "\n",The quality characteristic of house lager is defined by the liquid volume poured from the tap. This quantitative characteristic operates on a ratio scale. It is not binary data, and is monitored by calculating the means and ranges of five pours per shift.
    "### Q3: Briefly describe why a CUSUM and EWMA chart would be appropriate in this scenario, given the general manager's specific concern about the aging CO2 regulator. What are the limitations of these charts?\n",
    "\n",The manager is concerned about a small, gradual decrease in pour volume over time. A Shewhart chart looks at each shift separately, so it may miss small changes. CUSUM and EWMA use information from previous shifts, making them better at detecting slow, ongoing changes. In this data, the Shewhart chart showed no signals, but both CUSUM and EWMA detected a shift. However, these charts depend on good Phase I estimates, assume normal and independent data, and are less effective for large, sudden changes.
    "## Analyze\n",
    "\n",
    "### Q4: Read the Phase I data into R. Calculate and report the subgroup means and ranges, and use them to estimate the process mean and process standard deviation."
   ]Subgroup means and ranges (all 25 shifts, n = 5):Using all 25 subgroups: 
 x= 16.0386 oz, 
r = 0.1795 oz, so 
o^2 = 0.0772 oz. These are preliminary: they are inflated by the assignable causes
  },
