    "## Improve\n",
    "\n",
    "### Q8: The general manager wants a monitoring scheme that reliably detects a sustained shift of about one process standard deviation (representing early-stage regulator drift) while keeping the in-control ARL around 370-500. Using the design guidance from lecture, select and briefly justify: (a) the CUSUM slack value $K$ and decision interval $H$, and (b) the EWMA smoothing constant $\\lambda$ and width $L$, that you will carry forward into your Phase II monitoring plan.\n",
    "\n",For the CUSUM, I chose K = 0.015 oz and H = 0.150 oz. K is how far a shift’s average must fall from normal before it starts to count, and I set it to half of the drift we want to catch, which is the standard rule. H is the running total that triggers an alarm. This setup gives about one false alarm every 465 shifts, and it catches a one-standard-deviation drift in roughly 10 shifts.Both charts track shift averages, so I measured “one standard deviation” in terms of the average of five pours. A drift of one standard deviation in single pours would be caught even faster.
    "## Control\n",
    "\n",
    "### Q9: Read the Phase II data into R. Using your final Phase I estimates from Q6 and the CUSUM parameters you selected in Q9, construct the Phase II CUSUM chart. Does the chart signal an out-of-control condition? If so, at which shift number?"
   ]
  },Yes, the CUSUM chart signals, and it does so at shift 13. The CUSUM adds up how far each shift’s average falls below normal, and that total passed the alarm line of 0.150 oz at shift 13. It came close at shift 11, when it reached 0.147 oz, and it never signaled on the high side. The chart suggests the drift started around shift 7 and that the average has dropped to about 16.016 oz, which is roughly 0.04 oz below normal.
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "754e5fc4",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "## Q9 Code ##"
   ]
  },## Q9 Code ##
phase2 <- read_excel("BustersSportsBar_PhaseII.xlsx")
pours2 <- as.matrix(phase2[, paste0("Pour", 1:5)])
m2     <- nrow(pours2)
xbar2  <- rowMeans(pours2)

# Tabular CUSUM using Phase I estimates
Cplus <- Cminus <- numeric(m2)
cp_prev <- cm_prev <- 0
for (i in 1:m2) {
  cp_prev <- max(0, xbar2[i] - (mu_hat + K) + cp_prev)
  cm_prev <- max(0, (mu_hat - K) - xbar2[i] + cm_prev)
  Cplus[i] <- cp_prev
  Cminus[i] <- cm_prev
}
cusum_signal <- which(Cplus > H | Cminus > H)
cat("CUSUM first signal at shift:", ifelse(length(cusum_signal) == 0, "none", cusum_signal[1]), "\n")

# Chart: plot lower sum as negative so both sides share one axis
plot(1:m2, Cplus, type = "b", pch = 19, ylim = c(-1.3 * max(H, max(Cminus)), 1.3 * H),
     xlab = "Shift", ylab = "Cumulative sum (oz)", main = "Phase II CUSUM")
lines(1:m2, -Cminus, type = "b", pch = 19, col = "blue")
abline(h = c(-H, H), col = "red", lty = 2); abline(h = 0, col = "darkgreen")
points(cusum_signal, -Cminus[cusum_signal], col = "red", pch = 19, cex = 1.4)
legend("topright", c("C+", "-C-"), col = c("black", "blue"), pch = 19, bty = "n")

# Estimated start of drift and size once the lower side signals
N_minus <- sum(Cminus[1:cusum_signal[1]] > 0 & seq_len(cusum_signal[1]) > max(c(0, which(Cminus[1:cusum_signal[1]] == 0))))
mu_new  <- mu_hat - K - Cminus[cusum_signal[1]] / N_minus
cat(sprintf("Drift began ~shift %d; estimated new mean = %.4f oz\n",
            cusum_signal[1] - N_minus + 1, mu_new))
  {
