    "### Q11: Compare the results of Q9 and Q10. Which chart detected the shift more quickly, if either? Is this consistent with what you would expect, given what you know about the relative sensitivity of CUSUM and EWMA charts to small, sustained shifts? Briefly explain."
   ]
  },The EWMA flagged the problem first, at shift 11, and the CUSUM followed at shift 13. After that, both charts agreed. This is what I would expect, because both are designed for slow, steady drifts and perform about the same, so a gap of a couple of shifts is normal. The EWMA’s first alarm was also very marginal, so shift 13 is the better point to say that both charts agree.
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "480903db",
