   "source": [
    "### Q13: Provide an overview of the results that the general manager can provide to Buster's ownership. Does the CO2 regulator need to be replaced ahead of schedule, or can it run as planned?"
   ]
  }Measuring the pours right after the new CO2 regulator was installed, and then tracking 20 later shifts to see whether the aging regulator was pulling pours down. At the start, the tap poured about 16.055 oz on average, which is about 0.055 oz more than the 16.00 oz target. That meant about 1.5% of pours would fall outside the allowed range of 15.80 to 16.20 oz, almost all of them overpours that give away beer.

In the later data, both  monitoring charts show a real and steady downward drift in pour volume, which fits what we would expect from an aging regulator. The drift appears to have started around shift 7, and by the last eight shifts the average pour had fallen to about 16.00 oz. A regular average chart would have missed this change entirely, because no single shift looked unusual on its own.

For now, the drift has not caused a problem. It has actually moved the average pour closer to the target, and none of the 100 pours in the later data fell outside the specification limits. However, the drift is still continuing, so the average will likely keep sliding below 16.00 oz. If that happens, customers will start getting short pours with more foam, which leads to complaints and bad reviews.

My recommendation is that the regulator should not simply run as planned, although this is not an emergency. The regulator and CO2 supply should be inspected and recalibrated soon, and the regulator should be replaced at the next convenient maintenance window instead of on the original planned date. The monitoring charts should keep running in the meantime. The regulator should be replaced right away if the average pour drops below about 15.97 oz or if any individual pour falls below 15.80 oz. That trigger is my own suggestion, so you should adjust it if you think a different cutoff is more appropriate.
